26. Logs  hub

What happened on this machine, where it's written down, and how to keep it from
filling the disk.

On your machine
  Journal   on disk (/var/log/journal): kept across reboots · 300 B in 2 files
  Settings  SystemMaxUse=500M
  Syslog    rsyslog also writes text files in /var/log
  Rotation  2 rules in /etc/logrotate.d
  Biggest   /var/log/syslog 4.9 KiB · /var/log/nginx/access.log 2.9 KiB ·
            /var/log/auth.log 1.2 KiB

Commands
  journalctl -b -p warning  warnings and errors since boot
  journalctl -u nginx -f    follow one service's log
  journalctl -b -1 -e       how the previous boot ended
  journalctl --disk-usage   space the journal takes
  sudo dmesg -w             kernel messages as they arrive
  ls -lt /var/log | head    text logs written most recently

Paths
  /var/log/journal/           the journal, when it's kept on disk
  /var/log/                   text logs from rsyslog and programs
  /etc/systemd/journald.conf  journal storage and size limits
  /etc/logrotate.d/           rotation rules for text logs

Cards
  atfq 26.1  Reading the journal with journalctl
  atfq 26.2  Where logs go: journald, syslog and /var/log
  atfq 26.3  Log rotation and logs that fill the disk
  atfq 26.4  Logging from your own programs and services
  atfq 21.3  dmesg and the kernel log
